A Scott presentation records intersection equality and consistency as recursively decidable relations.

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`ScottPresentation`).** A Scott presentation of a neighbourhood system is a surjective numbering of its neighbourhoods for which equality of a numbered intersection with a third numbered neighbourhood, and the existence of a numbered neighbourhood below two given ones, are recursively decidable.

**Definition (`ComputableMapS`).** An approximable map between Scott-presented systems is computable when the relation between pairs of numbered basic neighbourhoods is recursively enumerable.

**Definition (`NeighborhoodSystem.IsEffectivelyGivenS`).** A neighbourhood system is effectively given in the Scott sense when it admits a Scott presentation.

**Definition (`ScottPresentation.inclShuffle`).** The inclusion shuffle sends a pair of indices \((n,m)\) to the triple \((n,m,n)\).

**Theorem (`ScottPresentation.primrec_inclShuffle`).** The inclusion shuffle is primitive recursive.

*Proof.* Pairing, both projections, and their compositions are primitive recursive. ∎

**Theorem (`ScottPresentation.incl_computable`).** Inclusion between numbered neighbourhoods of a Scott presentation is recursively decidable.

*Proof.* The inclusion \(X_n\subseteq X_m\) is equivalent to \(X_n\cap X_m=X_n\), an instance of decidable intersection equality. ∎

**Definition (`ScottPresentation.swapPair`).** The pair-swap operation sends \((n,m)\) to \((m,n)\).

**Theorem (`ScottPresentation.primrec_swapPair`).** Pair-swap is primitive recursive.

*Proof.* It is obtained by pairing the second and first projections. ∎

**Theorem (`ScottPresentation.eq_computable`).** Equality between numbered neighbourhoods of a Scott presentation is recursively decidable.

*Proof.* Two sets are equal exactly when each is included in the other, and both inclusions are decidable. ∎

**Definition (`ComputablePresentation.toScott`).** Every computable presentation has an underlying Scott presentation with the same numbering and decision procedures.

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.IsEffectivelyGiven.toS`).** Every effectively given neighbourhood system is effectively given in the Scott sense.

*Proof.* Forget the chosen intersection index and master index from a computable presentation. ∎
